<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nogais</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nogai people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nogais are a Turkic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and the steppes north of the Caspian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Seas</a>, traditionally named after Nogai, a 13th-century commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. From the 15th century they formed the Nogai Horde, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nomadic-confederation">nomadic confederation</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Ural, and later groups lived under the Crimean Khanate. Most now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavropol">Stavropol</a> Krai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; they are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nogais.jpg" alt="Nogais" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Polypone · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_r%C3%A9partition_noga%C3%AFs.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nogais are a Turkic people who speak Nogai, a language of the Kipchak branch. Their name comes from Nogai Khan, who died in 1299 or 1300, a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and a great-great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who was de facto ruler and kingmaker of the Horde and briefly proclaimed himself khan. The Nogais descend from Mongolic and Turkic tribes that formed the Nogai Horde, of which a Mongol tribe called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghits</a> was the core. They originated as nomadic tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and moved west after the Golden Horde conquered them in the 13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nogai Horde supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a>, and after the Russians conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> in 1556 it turned to the Crimean Khanate. The Nogais guarded its northern borders and, through raids into the Wild Fields, hindered Slavic settlement. A split came when Yusuf Mirza favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> while his brother Ismail Mirza, Bey of the Nogai Horde from 1555 to 1563, allied with the Russians. Yusuf's followers went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and Yedisan, and about 1630 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and expelled the Nogais from their homeland.</p><p>After the Russian annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1783, settlers occupied Nogai pastures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> resettled about 120,000 Nogais in the 1770s and 1780s, and the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> slaughtered thousands of rebellious Kuban Nogais in 1783. The government forced the rest to settle by burning their tents and limiting their movement. The great exodus came in 1860, when 50,000 of some 70,000 Nogais of Kuban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavropol">Stavropol</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Nogais raised herds and migrated seasonally in search of pasture, and were proud of nomadic traditions that they thought superior to settled farming. There are nine main groups, among them the Ak Nogai, Karagash and Bug-Nogai. Most live in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and Stavropol Krai, with others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a> and Astrakhan Oblast and smaller communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and Kazakhstan. Those in Turkey have kept dishes such as üken börek and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> boiled with milk, butter, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nogais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Circassian princes fought the Crimean Khanate, the <b>Nogais</b> and one another, and Kabarda allied with Muscovy in the 1550s; in 1708 Kabardian forces defeated a Crimean army, reportedly much larger, at the Battle of Kanzhal, ending Crimean claims to tribute from Kabarda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans fought the Shaybanids of Bukhara for the towns of the Syr Darya, the <b>Nogais</b> to the west and the Moghuls and Oirats to the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population of Tatars and <b>Nogais</b> lived largely by herding and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lesser <b>Nogais</b> were pastoral nomads who raided Muscovite and Caucasian lands, often in alliance with the Crimean Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/lesser-nogai-horde">Lesser Nogai Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Nogais</b> were pastoral nomads whose huge herds of horses were sold to Muscovy and Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/state/nogai-horde">Nogai Horde</a></li></ul></section>
